## Why This Is Asked
Assesses design for multi-tenant data observability, lineage, and controlled evolution. Focus on practical patterns and failure handling beyond basic ingestion.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation and schema evolution
- Exactly-once processing and idempotency
- End-to-end lineage with Data Catalog
- Rollback by time travel + Pub/Sub replay
- Cost and fault-tolerance considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: derive per-tenant lineage and offset tracking for replay
function processEvent(evt, tenant) {
  // ensure idempotent write
  const key = `${tenant}:${evt.id}`;
  if (store.has(key)) return;
  writeToBQ(tenant, evt);
  store.set(key, true);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback across tenants with partial data loss?
- How would you monitor data freshness and SLA adherence per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate's ability to implement a straightforward streaming ingestion with validation, error handling, and rollback using familiar GCP primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Pub/Sub ingestion and Dataflow (Beam)
- JSON schema validation and field filtering
- BigQueryIO writes with insertId for idempotency and daily partitioning
- Dead-letter handling and Cloud Storage archival
- Lightweight testing and rollback planning

## Code Example
```javascript
function validate(record){
  if (typeof record.user_id !== 'string') return false;
  if (typeof record.event_time !== 'string') return false;
  if (typeof record.event_type !== 'string') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late-arriving data and schema evolution?
- What monitoring and alerting would you add for bad records and rollback validation?
